    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\n [PEP 758](https://peps.python.org/pep-0758/) in Python 3.14 make `except A, B: / except* A, B:` a valid syntax, which should be equivalent to: `except (A, B):`.\nCython does not support this Syntax\n\n - `except A, B:` compiles without error but is treated as the legacy Python 2 form\n   `except A, B:` transforms into `except A as B:` and only A is caught and the exception instance is assigned\n   to the name B. This happens regardless of language_level=3.\n - `except* A, B:` is rejected with a syntax error.\n - `except A, B, C, ...:` (more than 2 exceptions) is rejected with a syntax error.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\nExample 1: UnboundLocalError\n```cython\ntry:\n    raise TypeError\nexcept ValueError, TypeError:\n    return \"error\"\n```\n\nExample 2: Syntax Error\n```cython\ntry:\n    raise ExceptionGroup(\"eg\", [ValueError(), TypeError()])\nexcept* ValueError, TypeError: \n    return \"error\"\n```\n\nExample 3: Syntax Error\n```cython\ntry:\n    raise TypeError\nexcept ValueError, TypeError, KeyError:\n    return \"error\"\n```\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nMatch CPython 3.14 / PEP 758:\n- `except A, B:` is equivalent to `except (A, B):`.\n- `except A, B, C:` is equivalent to `except (A, B, C):`\n- `except* A, B:` is equivalent to  `except* (A, B):`\n\n(and all other permutations of this syntax of course)\n\n### OS\n\nLinux, but I suspect every OS\n\n### Python version\n\n3.14.8\n\n### Cython version\n\n3.3.0\n\n### Additional context\n\nPEP: [PEP-758](https://peps.python.org/pep-0758/)",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "The big complication is that we still support the old Python 2 syntax, which conflicts with this, and is why you're getting `UnboundLocalError`.\n\nI think practically that means we should add a warning for the old Python 2 syntax as soon as possible (since there will be a surprising number of people still using it). But how to do the transition is a bit delicate.\n\nIn principle I think if you have 3 or more exception types that's unambiguous. And for known exception types the meaning is almost certainly pretty clear (i.e. `except ValueError, TypeError:` is almost always trying to catch a `ValueError` or `TypeError` but `except ValueError, a:` might still be an old-style assignment). I'm not sure if we could or should do much useful with that though.",
