            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "To add some context to this: I recently upgraded a Python Codebase to Python 3.14. \nThis Codebase is formatted using Ruff and when setting the Ruff Python Version to 314 it automatically enforces PEP758 style exceptions, leading to Cython Compilation Errors (luckily, because if there would have only been 2 exceptions per tuple, it might have gone unnoticed and lead to consequences down the road).\nAs a workaround I set Ruff to Python 3.13 Profile.\n\nIf you ask for my opinion:\nIf language level is 2, exceptions can be interpreted the way they are right now.\nIf language level is set to 3: The Cython Interpreter should conform to the Python 3 Syntax.\nThis means valid patterns are\n\n`except (ExceptionA, ExceptionB, ExceptionC) as e:`\n`except (ExceptionA, ExceptionB) as e:`\n`except ExceptionA, ExceptionB:`\n`except ExceptionA as e:`\n\nInvalid is the pattern like:\n`except ExceptionA, e:` which should be interpreted as `except ExceptionA as e:`\n\nBTW: It seems like the Cython Codebase is suffering from this ambigouity itself https://github.com/cython/cython/blob/master/Cython/Runtime/refnanny.pyx#L137.\n\n\n",
            "created_at": "2026-10-14T20:10:46Z",
